In compiled C#, `==` on two values of a datatype with type parameters is now true when they have the same constructor and equal arguments, also when the values were built at different type arguments, as the verifier assumes. For example, with `datatype Pair<+A, +B> = Pair(a: A, b: B)`, `Pair<Number, Integer>.Pair(i, j) == Pair<Integer, Number>.Pair(i, j)` was proved true and computed `false`, because the generated `Equals` cast its argument to the class of its receiver, whose type arguments differ. A value built at other type arguments is now compared, argument by argument, through a non-generic interface that each constructor class implements; a value built at the same type arguments is compared as before. Tuples, whose C# classes are in the runtime, are fixed the same way. The other backends compare datatype values by constructor and arguments only, and were not affected. The hidden option `--legacy-cs-datatype-equality` restores the old, unsound code generated for a program's own datatypes, for comparison and migration only (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/352).
